update public.posts set en = $c${"title":"Equivalence partitioning and boundary values: fewer test cases, still catching bugs","excerpt":"A number field can accept thousands of values, but you usually only need about 6 of them in the right places. These are the two techniques I use most when writing test cases.","tags":["Test case","Test techniques"],"content":"Faced with a \"Quantity: 1 to 1000\" field, nobody tests all 1000 numbers. The real question is which numbers to pick so you keep the test cases few without letting bugs slip through. The two most basic test techniques help answer that: equivalence partitioning and boundary value analysis.\n\n## Equivalence partitioning: split into groups, test one from each\n\nThe idea: values the system handles the same way belong to the same group, so testing one value stands in for the whole group.\n\nFor the \"1 to 1000\" field there are 3 groups:\n\n- **Valid:** 1 to 1000, try a number like 99.\n- **Invalid, too small:** try 0.\n- **Invalid, too large:** try 1001.\n\nDon't forget the \"edge\" groups that requirements rarely mention: leaving it empty, letters, negative numbers, decimals, special characters, pasting in a very long string.\n\n## Boundary values: bugs love the edges\n\nDevelopers often mix up \"less than\" and \"less than or equal to\". That's why bugs tend to sit right on the boundary between groups. For the \"1 to 1000\" field, the values worth trying are:\n\n- **0, 1, 2** around the lower boundary\n- **999, 1000, 1001** around the upper boundary\n\n## Applying it to real work\n\n- **Shopping cart:** max 5 of each product → try 0, 1, 5, 6. And try tapping the \"+\" button when you're already at 5.\n- **Discount code:** orders from 500,000 VND get a discount → try 499,999 VND, 500,000 VND, 500,001 VND. Remember to also check removing items so the order drops below the threshold.\n- **Blockchain wallet:** balance of 10 tokens, fee of 0.1 → try sending 9.9 (just enough), 9.91 (not enough to cover the fee), 0, and an amount with more decimal places than the token allows.\n- **Leave requests:** 2 days of leave left → try requesting 2 days, 2.5 days, and the last half day.\n\n## Quick tips\n\n- Boundaries aren't just numbers: string length (name up to 50 characters), dates (expires at 23:59), number of uploaded files, image file size.\n- Write the exact value you picked in the test case, not something vague like \"enter an invalid number\". Whoever reruns the test will know exactly what to type.\n\n## References\n\n- [What is Boundary Value Analysis and Equivalence Partitioning?](https://www.softwaretestinghelp.com/what-is-boundary-value-analysis-and-equivalence-partitioning/) — Software Testing Help"}$c$::jsonb where title = $c$Phân vùng tương đương và giá trị biên: ít test case mà vẫn bắt được bug$c$ and en = '{}'::jsonb;
update public.posts set en = $c${"title":"Smoke testing vs. sanity testing: how they differ and when to use each","excerpt":"These two names often get mixed up. A smoke test asks \"is this build worth testing further?\", while a sanity test asks \"is the thing that was just fixed working now?\"","tags":["Regression","Manual testing"],"content":"In meetings I often hear \"can you smoke test this for me\" and \"run sanity again\" as if they were the same thing. They're alike in that both are quick and both happen before deeper testing, but their purpose is completely different.\n\n## Smoke test: is the build \"smoking\"?\n\n- **When:** as soon as you get a new build, before anything else.\n- **Scope:** wide but shallow. Go through the whole system, touching each main feature once.\n- **Question to answer:** is this build stable enough to start testing?\n- **Outcome:** if it passes, keep testing; if it fails, send the build back to the devs so you don't waste a whole session testing a broken build.\n\nExample for an e-commerce site: the site opens, you can log in, the product list shows up, you can add to cart, the checkout page opens. About 10–15 minutes.\n\n## Sanity test: is the fix actually OK?\n\n- **When:** on an already stable build, after a bug fix or a small change.\n- **Scope:** narrow but deeper. Focus only on the part that changed and what's around it.\n- **Question to answer:** does this change work correctly and make sense?\n- **Outcome:** if it passes, move on to full regression or release; if it fails, reopen the bug.\n\nExample: a dev just fixed a bug where discount codes were applied incorrectly. I try a valid code, an expired code, and applying the same code twice at checkout, and check that the total is right. I don't retest the whole app.\n\n## Quick recap\n\n- **Smoke:** wide and shallow, for a new build, answers \"do we keep testing?\".\n- **Sanity:** narrow and focused, for a small change, answers \"is the fix OK?\".\n- Neither one replaces regression. Passing smoke or sanity only means it's ready for further testing, not that testing is done.\n\n## Tip for manual testers\n\nI keep a 10–15 line smoke checklist for each project, written in the order I move through the app. Every time a new build lands, I just run it top to bottom and mark each line pass or fail. This list is also the first candidate when the team starts on automation.\n\n## References\n\n- [Smoke Testing vs Sanity Testing](https://www.practitest.com/resource-center/article/smoke-testing-vs-sanity-testing/) — PractiTest"}$c$::jsonb where title = $c$Smoke test và sanity test: khác nhau ở đâu, khi nào dùng$c$ and en = '{}'::jsonb;
update public.posts set en = $c${"title":"RCRCRC: what to regression test when you're short on time","excerpt":"There's never enough time to retest everything before a release. RCRCRC is six letters that help me pick the right places to retest.","tags":["Regression","Manual testing"],"content":"Before every release, the hardest question isn't \"how do I test this?\" but \"what do I retest?\". The regression suite keeps getting longer, while the time stays the same. I often use RCRCRC, a heuristic coined by Karen N. Johnson, to decide what to retest.\n\n## 6 letters, 6 questions\n\n- **Recent:** what changed in this build? Read the release notes and the closed tickets, including \"small\" changes like a reworded message or a change to logging.\n- **Core:** which features would make users leave right away if they broke? For an e-commerce site that's search, cart, and checkout; for a wallet it's sending and receiving tokens.\n- **Risky:** which parts depend on external services, or on many other parts at once? Payment gateway, social login, data sync.\n- **Configuration sensitive:** which parts behave differently in the test environment and in production? Automated emails, time zones, API keys, blockchain network config.\n- **Repaired:** bugs that were just fixed, plus the features sitting right next to them. Fixing one thing and breaking another happens all the time.\n- **Chronic:** which areas seem to have bugs every few builds? Open the bug history in Jira and you'll spot them right away.\n\n## How I apply it\n\n1. Take the list of changes in the build and tag each item against the 6 letters above.\n2. Anything that hits 2 or more letters gets tested thoroughly first.\n3. **Core** always gets a quick pass, even when nobody touched it.\n4. Write down what I didn't get to and tell the team before the release. Don't stay silent.\n\n## Quick example\n\nThe build fixes a rounding bug in the wallet's transaction fees. Following RCRCRC, I'd retest: the send token screen (**Recent, Repaired**), the send and receive flows (**Core**), fees on testnet and mainnet (**Configuration sensitive**), and the transaction history, which used to show wrong amounts (**Chronic**).\n\nRCRCRC doesn't replace a set of test cases, but it helps me give a clear answer when someone asks \"why didn't you test that part?\".\n\n## References\n\n- [A software expert's heuristic for regression testing](https://www.techtarget.com/searchsoftwarequality/tip/A-software-experts-heuristic-for-regression-testing) — Karen N. Johnson, TechTarget\n- [RCRCRC — A heuristic for regression testing](https://www.ministryoftesting.com/moments/rcrcrc-a-heuristic-for-regression-testing) — Ministry of Testing"}$c$::jsonb where title = $c$RCRCRC: chọn gì để test regression khi không đủ thời gian$c$ and en = '{}'::jsonb;
update public.posts set en = $c${"title":"Exploratory testing with a plan: charter-based sessions in 90 minutes","excerpt":"Exploratory testing isn't random clicking. Splitting it into sessions with a goal, a time limit, and notes makes the results measurable and reportable.","tags":["Exploratory","Manual testing"],"content":"A lot of people think exploratory testing means \"there are no test cases, so just click around\". In reality, exploratory testing works best inside a clear structure. The approach I usually use is Session-Based Test Management (SBTM), developed by Jon Bach and James Bach.\n\n## Charter: one sentence that says what you're looking for\n\nEvery test session starts with a charter, a one-sentence goal. The template I usually use:\n\n> Explore **[area]** with **[tools, data, or approach]** to discover **[type of problem]**.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Explore **discount codes at checkout** with **expired codes, reused codes, and combined codes** to discover **incorrect totals**.\n- Explore **wallet recovery** with **mistyped recovery phrases and extra spaces** to discover **cases where the app shows the wrong message or hangs**.\n\nA charter gives you a direction; it doesn't make you follow steps one by one like a test case.\n\n## Timebox\n\nA session is usually around 90 minutes; it can be as short as 45 minutes or as long as 2 hours. When time's up, stop, even if you're on a roll. The timebox keeps me focused and helps the team estimate: \"6 more sessions to go for checkout\".\n\n## Take notes while you test\n\nI keep short notes in a file, one entry per session, for example:\n\n- **Charter:** discount codes at checkout\n- **Duration:** 90 minutes\n- **Bugs found:** 2\n- **Questions to ask:** can codes be stacked with other promotions?\n- **Follow-up session needed:** yes, refunds when an order is canceled\n\nThe \"Questions\" column matters just as much as the bugs column. A lot of what I find isn't a bug, it's an unclear requirement.\n\n## Debrief after the session\n\nAfter each session, I spend 15–30 minutes walking the team lead or a dev through it: what I tested, what I didn't get to, what got in my way, and what the next session should cover. That way exploratory testing produces clear results instead of \"I'm pretty sure it was tested thoroughly\".\n\n## References\n\n- [Session-Based Test Management](https://ryancraventech.substack.com/p/session-based-test-management) — Ryan Craven\n- [Session-Based Test Management](https://www.virtuosoqa.com/post/session-based-test-management) — Virtuoso QA"}$c$::jsonb where title = $c$Exploratory testing có kế hoạch: test theo charter trong 90 phút$c$ and en = '{}'::jsonb;
update public.posts set en = $c${"title":"Risk-based testing: prioritizing tests when the release date won't move","excerpt":"With a fixed deadline, testing everything is impossible. Scoring each area for impact and likelihood of failure helps decide what to test first and how deep to go.","tags":["Risk-based","Test planning"],"content":"The release date is locked in, and the test suite isn't even half done. At that point the right question is: if there's only time for half, which half is most worth testing? Risk-based testing helps answer that with numbers instead of gut feeling.\n\n## Risk = impact × likelihood\n\n**Impact if it breaks:** give each feature a score.\n\n- 3: catastrophic (lost money, lost data, wrong payroll)\n- 2: damaging (users can't continue)\n- 1: annoying (users need a workaround)\n- 0: just a nuisance (misaligned text, wrong color)\n\nThen add points for how often it's used: features everyone has to go through (login, checkout) weigh more than features few people use.\n\n**Likelihood of bugs:** watch for these signs.\n\n- Complex code with lots of conditions\n- Rushed changes right before the release\n- New technology the team isn't familiar with yet\n- Areas with a lot of past bugs\n- Many people changing the same thing, with unclear communication\n\n## Scoring and ranking\n\nI make a table in Google Sheets: one row per feature, score each factor from 1 to 5, multiply by its weight, and add it all up. It doesn't need to be perfectly precise; the goal is to sort things into high, medium, and low.\n\n- **Checkout + discount codes:** high impact, high likelihood (just changed) → test first, test deep.\n- **Product search:** medium impact, low likelihood → one quick pass.\n- **About page:** low impact, low likelihood → leave it for last.\n\n## Scheduling tests by score\n\n1. Do a quick pass through the whole system first to see what \"smells off\".\n2. Test in depth in score order, from highest to lowest.\n3. Report serious bugs as soon as you find them; don't save them up for the end of the day. Reporting early leaves time to fix them or move the schedule.\n4. When time's up, stop and clearly report which parts weren't tested, along with their risk level.\n\nThat last point is the most important: testers don't decide whether to release, but we do have to make sure the people who decide know what risks they're accepting.\n\n## References\n\n- [Risk Based Testing — Strategies for Prioritizing Tests Against Deadlines](https://www.logigear.com/blogs/test-methods/Risk-Based-TestingStrategies-for-Prioritizing-Tests-Against-Deadlines) — LogiGear\n- [Risk-based Testing to Prioritize QA Efforts](https://testerwork.com/risk-based-testing-to-prioritize-qa-efforts/) — Testerwork"}$c$::jsonb where title = $c$Test theo rủi ro: xếp thứ tự test khi ngày phát hành không dời được$c$ and en = '{}'::jsonb;
update public.posts set en = $c${"title":"SFDIPOT: 7 angles so you don't miss test cases","excerpt":"When I'm reading about a new feature and don't know where to start, I walk through the seven letters of SFDIPOT. Each letter is an angle that opens up cases that are easy to forget.","tags":["Heuristic","Test case"],"content":"When I write test cases for a new feature, I tend to get \"stuck\" on the happy path: enter valid input, click the button, see the result. SFDIPOT is a mnemonic from James Bach's Heuristic Test Strategy Model that helps you look at a feature from 7 sides.\n\n## The 7 letters\n\n- **S – Structure:** what is the product made of? Screens, APIs, config files, user guides.\n- **F – Function:** what does it do? Calculations, permissions, error handling, startup and shutdown.\n- **D – Data:** what data does it take in and give back? Empty data, data that's too long, wrong formats, old data still lying around.\n- **I – Interfaces:** how do users and other systems touch it? The UI, APIs, file import and export.\n- **P – Platform:** what external things does it depend on? Browsers, iOS/Android versions, third-party services.\n- **O – Operations:** who uses it, and how? New users, experienced users, users who are deliberately trying to break it.\n- **T – Time:** what happens over time? Rapid repeated clicks, a slow network, two people editing at once, a login session expiring.\n\n## Trying it out: a \"request leave\" feature\n\nHere's an example from an HR management app I used to test:\n\n- **S:** the leave request form, the manager's approval screen, the notification email.\n- **F:** deducting leave days, multi-level approval, rejecting with a reason.\n- **D:** half-day leave, leave that spans a weekend, requesting leave after running out of days.\n- **I:** exporting an Excel report, the API that syncs to the timesheet system.\n- **P:** using it on a phone, the Safari browser.\n- **O:** if a department head requests leave, who approves it? What if an employee edits a request while it's pending approval?\n- **T:** two approval levels click at the same moment; a request is still pending when the new year starts, so which year's leave does it count against?\n\nThe letter **T** alone gave me two cases that the happy path would never reach.\n\n## A quick tip\n\nYou don't need all 7 letters for every feature. I use SFDIPOT as a checklist of questions to ask myself for 10 minutes before writing test cases, then pick the riskiest angles.\n\n## References\n\n- [Heuristic Test Strategy Model](https://developsense.com/htsm) — James Bach, Satisfice (published on DevelopSense)"}$c$::jsonb where title = $c$SFDIPOT: 7 góc nhìn để không bỏ sót trường hợp test$c$ and en = '{}'::jsonb;
update public.posts set en = $c${"title":"Ask before you test: the tester in a Three Amigos session","excerpt":"The cheapest bug is the one that never gets written into code. Sitting down with the PO and dev before work starts, and asking questions with concrete examples, gets the whole team on the same page.","tags":["Three Amigos","Requirements"],"content":"A lot of the bugs I find aren't really coding mistakes. They happen because the dev and the PO understood the requirement differently. The cheapest way to catch this kind of bug is to ask questions before any work starts, in a short session called Three Amigos.\n\n## What is Three Amigos\n\nThree perspectives sit down together before a user story is worked on:\n\n- **Product Owner / BA:** what problem we want to solve.\n- **Developer:** how to build it.\n- **Tester:** what could go wrong.\n\nIt doesn't have to be exactly 3 people. What matters is having all 3 perspectives in the room.\n\n## Example Mapping: asking with examples\n\nThe format I've found most effective is Matt Wynne's Example Mapping. It uses cards in 4 colors:\n\n- **Yellow:** the user story\n- **Blue:** rules\n- **Green:** concrete examples for each rule\n- **Red:** questions nobody can answer yet\n\nEach story should take about 25 minutes. If the table fills up with red cards, the story isn't ready to work on. If one rule has too many green cards, the story should be split into smaller ones.\n\n## Example: \"Apply a discount code\"\n\n- **Rule:** only one code per order. **Example:** code A is already applied and the customer enters code B → does B replace A, or do we show an error?\n- **Rule:** expired codes can't be used. **Example:** the code expires at 23:59 and the customer clicks checkout at 23:59:30 → do we go by the time they clicked, or the time payment completed?\n- **Question (red card):** if an order is returned, does the customer get the code usage back?\n\nEach green example is practically a ready-made test case, and each red card is a bug stopped before any code exists.\n\n## What testers should ask\n\n- \"What if…?\" for boundary data: empty, zero, very large, exactly at the limit.\n- \"Who shouldn't be allowed to do this?\" to ask about permissions.\n- \"What does the user see when something fails?\" to ask about error messages.\n- \"How do we know it's done?\" to pin down the acceptance criteria.\n\n## References\n\n- [Introducing Example Mapping](https://cucumber.io/blog/bdd/example-mapping-introduction/) — Matt Wynne, Cucumber\n- [Example Mapping: The Three Amigos](https://www.qatouch.com/qa-masterclass/example-mapping-the-three-amigos) — QA Touch\n- [The key role testing plays in a Three Amigos strategy](https://www.techtarget.com/searchsoftwarequality/tip/The-key-role-testing-plays-in-a-Three-Amigos-strategy) — TechTarget"}$c$::jsonb where title = $c$Hỏi trước khi test: tester trong buổi Three Amigos$c$ and en = '{}'::jsonb;
update public.posts set en = $c${"title":"Writing bug reports devs actually want to read","excerpt":"How to structure the title, steps to reproduce, expected result and attached evidence so devs can fix things fast without coming back to ask.","tags":["Bug report","Manual testing"],"content":"A good bug report helps the dev fix the issue quickly without having to come back with questions. This is the structure I always use.\n\n## 1. A title that says exactly what happened\nThe title should answer: **where, what you did, and what went wrong**. For example: \"Cart: applying a discount code twice makes the total negative\".\n\n## 2. Steps to reproduce\n- Write it step by step, one action per step\n- Note the exact data you used (account, discount code, product)\n- Note the environment: browser or device, app version\n\n## 3. Actual result and expected result\nPut these two lines side by side so the dev can see right away what's wrong.\n\n## 4. Evidence\nScreenshots, a short video, errors from DevTools, or the API response in Postman. A 15-second video is often worth more than a long description.\n\n> Note: this is a sample post, edit it in Admin → Blog."}$c$::jsonb where title = $c$Viết bug report dev nào cũng muốn đọc$c$ and en = '{}'::jsonb;
update public.posts set en = $c${"title":"Writing test cases that are enough, not too much","excerpt":"How I break down requirements, choose which cases to test, and write test cases that anyone can pick up and run.","tags":["Test case","Manual testing"],"content":"Test cases that are too thin let bugs slip through; test cases that are too long, nobody wants to read. Here's how I strike the balance.\n\n## Start from the requirements\n- Read the requirements carefully and list out each business rule\n- Ask about anything vague before you write, don't guess\n\n## Choose what to test\n- **Happy path**: the user does exactly what's expected\n- **Boundary values**: the minimum, the maximum, and just over the limit\n- **Invalid data**: empty fields, wrong formats, special characters\n- **Business edge cases**: out of stock, expired, insufficient permissions\n\n## What a good test case has\n1. A short title that says clearly what's being checked\n2. Preconditions (the account and data you need)\n3. Steps to perform\n4. A specific, verifiable expected result\n\n> Note: this is a sample post, edit it in Admin → Blog."}$c$::jsonb where title = $c$Viết test case sao cho đủ mà không thừa$c$ and en = '{}'::jsonb;
update public.posts set en = $c${"title":"Testing blockchain wallets: bugs new testers often miss","excerpt":"The cases worth testing thoroughly when testing wallets and NFTs on mobile, drawn from the launcher project I'm working on.","tags":["Blockchain","Mobile"],"content":"Testing a blockchain wallet is different from testing a regular app: one wrong step can affect the user's assets. These are the spots I've found easy to miss.\n\n## Always test on a test network\nUse **testnet** and test tokens, never real money. State which network you used in every bug report.\n\n## Creating and restoring a wallet\n- Restore a wallet with a seed phrase entered in the wrong order, with extra spaces, or with typos\n- Uninstall and reinstall the app, then restore the wallet and check that the balance and history are correct\n\n## Sending and receiving tokens\n- Wrong wallet address, insufficient balance, an amount of zero\n- Losing the connection mid-send: does the transaction get sent twice?\n- Do the pending, successful and failed states display correctly?\n\n## Displaying NFTs\n- Broken or slow-loading NFT images\n- Collections with many NFTs, names that are too long\n\n> Note: this is a sample post, edit it in Admin → Blog."}$c$::jsonb where title = $c$Test ví blockchain: những lỗi tester mới hay bỏ sót$c$ and en = '{}'::jsonb;
